/-!
# Exact images and full fixed seeds of escaping plane radius kernels

In the original cubic plane graph, an escaping radius subactor containing
the critical starting seed has endpoint image of order exactly four. The
full last-edge radius kernel has the same image size. The critical seed
still has image of order two; the cubic projection retains kernel E,
including the nontrivial odd quotient E/Q.

For the upper bound, transport the proved alignment/control dichotomy to
the literal six-letter cover. Escape rules out control. A fixed test word
then restricts the aligned actor to the four-group generated by the critical
transposition and the complementary double transposition. The calculation
uses the actual natural quotient, so it applies to both moving covers.
The nonzero fixed-kernel commutator rules out an image of order two.

Every four-element image in the plane edge has moving fixed index four.
The whole seed's central attachment is fixed by the edge; comparison of
indices therefore identifies its full fixed subgroup with the actual edge
omega-center. At distance four with uniformly small critical images, these
facts apply to the already selected escaping midpoint kernel and its whole
last-edge radius kernel, retaining the same noncentral aligned line.

The whole-core and literal C.1.32 applications retain both covers, the Borel
alternative and the original radical. The exact indices and fixed seeds
are not a terminal contradiction. The generating-stabilizer comparison,
remaining image/distance cases and both final core exhaustions remain.

Source: Meierfrankenfeld--Stellmacher, Pushing up weak BN-pairs of rank two
(1993), (3.3)(ii) and the fixed-kernel comparison in (5.12)(1), printed
pp.866--867,924--925; Aschbacher--Smith C.1.31--32, printed pp.135--136.
-/

